insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-b-problem', 1, 'Write the reciprocal of the instantaneous decline, 1/D(t), against time for a hyperbolic well. What does the decline exponent b correspond to on that plot?', '["The intercept at t = 0, which reads 500 days for Ekene-3", "The curvature of the line, which grows as the well moves from transient into boundary-dominated flow", "The slope of the line, so Ekene-3 traces 500 + 0.5t", "The point where the line crosses 0.518091009909798"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Inverting the hyperbolic decline gives 1/D(t) = 1/Di + b*t, a straight line whose intercept is 1/Di and whose slope is b. The intercept carries Di rather than b, the loss ratio of a true hyperbolic has no curvature at all, and 0.518091009909798 is Ekene-3''s tangent effective annual decline, which has nothing to do with this plot.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-b-problem', 2, 'Differentiating the hyperbolic decline gives dD/dt = -b*D^2. Which statement about the three Arps parameters follows from that relationship?', '["b is a first-order feature exactly like Di, so both are fixed by the same two rate readings", "b is the slope of the reciprocal of a slope, a second-order feature of the rate history, so it is the least identifiable of the three parameters", "b sets the level of the history as qi does, so one accurate rate reading constrains it", "b is the only parameter carrying units, so it is the most sensitive to days versus years"]'::jsonb, 1, 'qi is a level, Di is a slope, and b is the slope of the reciprocal of a slope. Each step up in order costs signal, which is why b moves reserves the most and is pinned down by data the least. b is dimensionless, so the choice of time unit is not the issue.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-b-problem', 3, 'Ekene-3 has Di = 0.002 per day and b = 0.5, so its instantaneous decline at 365 days is 0.00146520146520147 per day. What is its loss ratio 1/D at that age?', '["682.5 days, matching the loss ratio line 500 + 0.5t evaluated at 365 days", "1018 days, its loss ratio at 1036 days", "1127.75 days, Ekene-6''s loss ratio at one year", "833.333333333333 days, Ekene-1''s constant loss ratio"]'::jsonb, 0, '1/0.00146520146520147 = 682.5, and the loss ratio line 500 + 0.5*365 gives the same value. The 1018 day figure is Ekene-3''s loss ratio at 1036 days, 1127.75 is Ekene-6''s at one year, and 833.333333333333 is Ekene-1''s constant loss ratio.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-b-problem', 4, 'A colleague proposes carrying a fitted b of 0.35 from Ekene-6 to every new well in the field, on the grounds that b is a property of the rock. What is wrong with that reasoning?', '["A fitted b describes the assumed economic limit, so it carries only between wells abandoned at 10 stb/d", "A fitted b describes the fluid, so it carries only between wells in the same fluid and must then be rescaled by the formation volume factor", "A fitted b describes measurement scatter, so it carries only between wells whose allocation procedures give comparable noise", "A fitted b describes a flow regime seen through a window, and both change over a well''s life"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Transient linear flow, transient radial flow and boundary-dominated flow carry different characteristic exponents in the same rock, and a well moves between them as it depletes. Change the drive mechanism, as the Ekene flood does on 2023-01-01, and the fitted exponent stops describing rock behaviour at all.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-b-problem', 5, 'How does the engine find the hyperbolic exponent, and on what criterion does it choose?', '["It solves for b by Newton iteration on the linearised residual and stops when successive estimates agree to within 0.001", "It sweeps b in steps of 0.05 and keeps the rung with the lowest RMSE on the rate scale", "It sweeps b in steps of 0.05 and keeps the rung with the highest R2, which is why two wrong families both land in the Excellent band", "It fits the exponential and the harmonic first, then interpolates b between them using the ratio of their RMSE values"]'::jsonb, 1, 'At each rung the engine runs the linearised regression, back-transforms into qi and Di, rebuilds the predicted rates and computes RMSE. Lowest RMSE wins within the grid, and Auto-Select then ranks that survivor against the exponential and harmonic candidates on RMSE as well. There is no Newton solve and no interpolation in the path.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-b-problem', 6, 'Ekene-3''s fitted exponent is reported as 0.49999999999999994 rather than 0.5. Why?', '["The regression converged to within 5.551115123125783e-17 of 0.5 and stopped at the engine''s tolerance", "The rate data carries rounding at the fifteenth digit", "The grid rungs are accumulated by repeated addition of 0.05, which is not exact in binary", "The engine reports the midpoint of the winning bin rather than the rung itself, and that midpoint falls one unit in the last place below 0.5"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Adding 0.05 to itself ten times gives 0.49999999999999994 while multiplying 0.05 by 10 gives 0.5, because 0.05 has no exact binary representation, and the engine uses the accumulating loop. Ekene-6''s seventh rung lands on exactly 0.35, so there is no predictable pattern to which rungs come out tidy.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-b-problem', 7, 'The grid loop skips any rung within 0.001 of 1. What is the practical consequence when you force the Hyperbolic model on Ekene-5, whose planted exponent is exactly 1?', '["The best the grid can reach is 0.9500000000000003, which under-books the EUR by 3.35719635017790 percent", "The fit fails to converge and the engine falls back to the exponential candidate", "The engine substitutes the harmonic result silently, returning qi 100.000000000000 under a Hyperbolic label", "The grid runs past the skipped rung to 1.0500000000000003, one step above the true exponent"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The harmonic has its own exact linearisation and is handled as a separate candidate, so the grid passes over the rung at 1. Forced to the hyperbolic family, Ekene-5 comes back at R2 0.999934526382689 and RMSE 0.133888840068019 stb/d, booking 148352.186019487 stb at a 10 stb/d limit against the correct 153505.672866270 stb.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-b-problem', 8, 'The engine''s default maxB is 2 and the grid is accumulated by repeated addition. What is the highest exponent the grid can actually report?', '["2.000000000000001, because the loop condition is checked after the addition rather than before it", "1.9500000000000006, because the loop deliberately stops one full step short of maxB", "2, because the condition is inclusive and the sequence reaches exactly 2 after thirty-nine additions", "1.950000000000001, because the next accumulated value, 2.000000000000001, fails the b <= 2 test"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The accumulated sequence reaches 1.950000000000001 and the following value is 2.000000000000001, which fails the test, so b = 2 is never evaluated. That makes 1.95 the ceiling in practice, and a reported 1.95 means the optimizer wanted more range rather than that it chose that value from alternatives.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-b-problem', 9, 'Displacing Ekene-3''s exponent from 0.5 to 0.6 costs 0.0543482785185351 stb/d of RMSE over six monthly rows and 0.991015196328253 stb/d over thirty-four. What does the growth show?', '["The error in b grows as the window lengthens, because grid rounding compounds", "The evidence against a wrong b grows with elapsed time while the error itself stays at 0.1", "The engine weights later rows more heavily, pushing the regression toward the tail", "The rate data becomes noisier as the well ages, so a longer window carries more scatter at every candidate exponent"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The exponent error is identical in both cases. Only the misfit it produces has grown, by a factor of 18.2345278147177. Identifiability is the ratio between that misfit and the scatter the data already carries, and the Ekene data carries none, so the penalty here is pure signal.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-b-problem', 10, 'Take a working field noise floor of 0.5 stb/d and apply it to Ekene-3''s first six monthly rows. Which grid rungs does the data fail to rule out, and what EUR range at a 10 stb/d limit do they span?', '["b from 0.25 to 0.75, spanning 93166.5399421189 to 137156.626155241 stb across eleven surviving rungs", "b from 0.45 to 0.55, spanning 108964.254599326 to 113719.588669943 stb, the only three rungs that ever survive on this well", "b from 0.05 to 1.35, spanning 77632.6753339386 to 298835.535826654 stb", "b from 0.05 to 1.95, the entire grid, because six rows exclude no candidate exponent at all"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Twenty-six rungs sit below a 0.5 stb/d misfit on six rows, and the booked volumes across them differ by nearly a factor of four. The 0.25 to 0.75 band belongs to the twelve row window and the three-rung band to the twenty-four and thirty-four row windows, both far tighter than six months of history allows.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-b-problem', 11, 'Analyst A has 200 daily rate points spanning 60 days. Analyst B has 34 monthly points spanning 1036 days. Whose data better determines b, and why?', '["B, because the loss ratio travels with elapsed time rather than with sample count", "A, because 200 points give a far smaller standard error on every regression coefficient", "A, because daily sampling resolves the early curvature where the families differ most", "Neither, because the exponent follows from the ratio of first rate to last rate"]'::jsonb, 0, 'What determines the exponent is how far 1/D has travelled from its intercept, and that travel is measured in calendar time. Ekene-3''s loss ratio moves from 500 to 1018 over 1036 days; compressed into 60 days it would move from 500 to 530. Sampling density buys precision on qi and Di, and only elapsed time buys the exponent.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-b-problem', 12, 'Across every forced wrong-family fit of the four Ekene producers, one relationship holds without exception. What is it?', '["The booking error scales with the b error at roughly fifty percent of EUR per unit of exponent", "The booking error is always positive, because a wrong family has to raise qi to track the history", "The booking error stays below the R2 shortfall expressed as a percentage, which makes the quality tier a usable screen", "The sign of the booking error matches the sign of the b error, over-booking whenever b is forced high"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Force the exponent too low and you buy a thin tail and under-book; force it too high and you buy a fat tail and over-book. Ekene-5 forced exponential comes out 41.2419399441714 percent light and Ekene-1 forced harmonic 51.4939822612476 percent heavy. Magnitude depends on Di and on how far the limit sits below qi, so only the sign is predictable.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-b-problem', 13, 'Ekene-6''s primary window forced to the Harmonic model returns qi 93.6153810033389 stb/d and Di 0.00133821021526847 per day at R2 0.993488578081531. What does that fit book at a 10 stb/d limit, against the correct 105266.626461929 stb?', '["89624.5162700159 stb, an under-booking of 14.8595150406673 percent", "156463.504642519 stb, an over-booking of 48.6354316665648 percent, from (qi/Di)*ln(qi/10)", "105266.626461929 stb, unchanged, because the two closed forms agree once the fitted parameters absorb the difference", "298759.978747020 stb, the figure this well books once its full production history is admitted to the fit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The harmonic EUR is 69955.6616256723 * 2.23660960394805. The forced fit had to raise qi from the true 90 stb/d and raise Di from the planted 0.001 per day to track the history, and the logarithm, which has no ceiling, then spends the tail. The R2 sits in the Excellent band throughout.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-b-problem', 14, 'To read Ekene-1''s naive full-history R2, which the Professional capstone asks for, what exactly do you set in the panel?', '["Full history with the Hyperbolic model forced and maxB tightened to 1.5, giving 0.809309589113655", "Primary window with Auto-Select and default constraints, giving R2 1.00000000000000", "Full history, Auto-Select, constraints left at their defaults, giving R2 0.818388421218434", "Full history with Auto-Select and maxB tightened to 1.0, giving R2 0.805676381435128"]'::jsonb, 2, 'All 72 monthly rows from 2020-01-01 to 2025-12-01, with nothing constrained. The fit comes back Hyperbolic with qi 97.2058663778433 stb/d, Di 0.00196150586036441 per day and b at the ceiling rung of 1.950000000000001, quality tier Fair. The other two values quoted come from deliberately constrained refits.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-b-problem', 15, 'Faced with a full-history Ekene-1 fit reporting b at the ceiling, an analyst tightens maxB to 1.0 and refits. What happens?', '["R2 falls to 0.805676381435128 and the alarm is silenced without being fixed", "R2 rises to 0.920390851618676 and the fit becomes defensible on physical grounds", "The engine returns the same hyperbolic parameters, since maxB bounds only the reported value", "The fit switches to Exponential and recovers qi 120.000000000000 with Di 0.00120000000000000, the true family surviving alone"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Auto-Select returns Harmonic with qi 88.2403213595711 stb/d and Di 0.000924465609791911 per day, slightly worse than the unconstrained fit. Tighten instead to 1.5 and force the hyperbolic and the exponent pins against the new ceiling at 1.4500000000000006. A b bound polices legitimate single-regime fits; it cannot repair a window spanning two drive mechanisms.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-fit-windows', 1, 'At Professional level, setting a fit window is understood as making three assertions at once. What are they?', '["One economic limit, one forecast horizon and one reporting category, so the booking traces to a single set of commercial assumptions", "One model family, one goodness-of-fit threshold and one exponent bound, all agreed before any data is loaded", "One well, one product stream and one reporting month, fixing the row set the regression sees", "One regime, one stream and one operating policy, all held across every row inside the boundary"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The window claims that a single physical drive acted across every row, that a single fluid stream is being described, and that choke, lift and offtake did not change inside the boundary. Limits, horizons and fit thresholds are downstream choices that do not affect which rows are entitled to be in the fit.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-fit-windows', 2, 'Fit Ekene-1 from 2021-01-01 to 2022-12-01 rather than from first oil. What happens to the reported qi and Di?', '["qi becomes 77.3462227639957 stb/d, the model rate on the window''s own start date, and Di stays at 0.00120000000000000 per day", "qi rises to 155.774959394670 stb/d and Di rises to 0.00308009452751842 per day", "Both are unchanged, since t = 0 is anchored to the well''s first production date", "qi stays at 120.000000000000 stb/d while Di falls, the line being extrapolated back to first oil"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The engine measures time from the first row inside the window, so fitted qi is the model rate on the window''s start date, which the fixture records as 77.34622276399577 stb/d. The decline constant does not move at all, because the physics did not change. The 155.77 and 0.00308 figures come from a forced harmonic fit of the primary window.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-fit-windows', 3, 'An analyst selects the window that maximises R2 and calls the result the best available fit. Why is that procedure unsound?', '["It ignores RMSE, which is the criterion Auto-Select applies when ranking the three candidate families against one another", "It optimises a statistic that the window itself produces, so the statistic ends up choosing the physics of the fit", "It cannot be reproduced by another analyst, since R2 depends on sampling frequency as well as on the boundaries", "It always selects the longest window, because R2 rises monotonically as rows are added to any least-squares fit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'R2 is computed from whatever rows the window admits, so choosing the window to maximise it is circular. Worse, the procedure has a bias: it trims early rows, which carry the most curvature and the most leverage, and trimming them flattens the apparent decline and raises the booking. Windows come from knowledge of the well, and the statistics are read afterwards as a check.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-fit-windows', 4, 'Ekene-1''s base rate at the flood start is 32.210476049246076 stb/d and its lift factor is 1.28, with a lag of 5 months and a 6 month ramp. What rate does its ramp end at, and when?', '["41.2294093430350 stb/d on 2023-06-01, six months after the flood start, the ramp beginning on the injection date itself", "45.2319930832442 stb/d on 2024-01-01, the value the response model returns for this base rate and lift", "41.2294093430350 stb/d on 2023-12-01, eleven months after injection began, once lag and ramp complete", "33.4312717799524 stb/d on 2023-07-01, the rate the well last recorded before injection began"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Gross ramps to base times lift over six months beginning after the lag expires, so the peak is 32.210476049246076 * 1.28 and it arrives 11 months after the 2023-01-01 flood start. The fixture''s 2023-12-01 row reads 41.22940934303498 stb/d. The 45.23 figure is Ekene-3''s ramp end and 33.43 is Ekene-1''s December 2022 row.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-fit-windows', 5, 'Why is a full-history Arps fit of a flooded Ekene producer described as invalid rather than merely poor?', '["Because every Arps member is strictly decreasing, so no choice of parameters produces flat and rising rows", "Because the grid cannot reach an exponent above 1.950000000000001, putting the required curvature outside the search range", "Because the R2 falls into the Fair or Poor band, and anything below the 0.95 Excellent threshold is disqualified", "Because the fitted qi comes out below the measured opening rate, and a fit that misses day one is rejected by convention"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Differentiating the general hyperbolic gives dq/dt = -qi*Di/(1 + b*Di*t)^(1+1/b), which is negative for every admissible qi, Di and b. Ekene-1 holds six consecutive rows at exactly 32.210476049246076 stb/d and then climbs, and no parameters produce that shape. It is a structural impossibility rather than a question of fit quality.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-fit-windows', 6, 'The naive full-history Auto-Select fit of Ekene-6 returns qi 87.9980156583808 stb/d, Di 0.000960001821405101 per day and b 1.3500000000000005. What makes this the most instructive of the four naive fits?', '["It is the only one whose fitted decline exceeds the planted decline, identifying it as contaminated", "Its qi and Di land within 2.3 and 4.0 percent of the planted 90 stb/d and 0.001 per day while the exponent is wrong by nearly a factor of four", "Its R2 of 0.780507944377468 is the highest of the four, so it survives a screen based on goodness of fit", "It is the only one Auto-Select returns as an Exponential, the flood hiding the well''s hyperbolic character"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Two of the three parameters look like a competent fit. The third is the one that sets the tail, and it is off by a large multiple. Reviewing fitted parameters one at a time will not catch this. Ekene-6''s naive R2 is in fact the second lowest of the four, and the fit comes back Hyperbolic like the other three.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-fit-windows', 7, 'Among the four naive full-history fits, Ekene-3 returns the highest R2 at 0.899873903499416. What does that tell you about R2 as a portfolio screen for regime changes?', '["It ranks wells by contamination severity, so the lowest scorers are the ones needing a window review first", "It works only for exponential wells, the log transform being the one linearisation whose residuals stay comparable across a regime change", "It is reliable provided the threshold is set at the Fair boundary of 0.80, which all four naive fits fall below", "It is unreliable, since the best-scoring well carries a Di 87 percent too high and an exponent at the grid ceiling"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Screening a portfolio by naive R2 and reviewing only the low scorers would let Ekene-3 through, and its fitted parameters are among the worst in the set. Two of the four naive fits sit above 0.80, so a Fair threshold does not rescue the screen either. Regime changes are found by knowing what happened to the well.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-fit-windows', 8, 'Fitted to its primary window with Auto-Select, Ekene-6 returns which nominal decline?', '["0.000960001821405101 per day, the value that emerges once all 64 of the well''s monthly rows enter the regression", "0.00114387174233099 per day, the decline of its oil stream once water cut begins to climb after breakthrough", "0.00100000000000000 per day", "0.000350000000000003 per day, the flood''s own constant, adopted once the response ramp has completed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The primary window recovers the planted 0.001 per day exactly, which is the value the Professional capstone grades to a tolerance of 0.00002. The three alternatives are all real Ekene-6 numbers in the wrong role: the naive full-history fit, the post-breakthrough oil fit and the post-ramp gross fit respectively.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-fit-windows', 9, 'Ekene-6''s primary-window fit reports qi as 89.9999999999999 stb/d rather than 90. How should that be read?', '["As a small but real misfit, the last digit of a fitted parameter being the engine''s estimate of its own precision", "A round-trip artefact of the power transform, given an RMSE alongside it of 4.31997628804008e-14 stb/d", "As evidence that one monthly row sits slightly off the planted curve", "As the effect of the exponent landing on 0.35, which biases qi downward by a fraction of a barrel"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The hyperbolic fit raises the rate to a power and back again, and the last digit does not always survive. Whether a misfit is real is judged in barrels per day by the RMSE tile, not by how tidy the digits look. A misfit worth worrying about would be visible in rate units rather than in the fifteenth digit.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-fit-windows', 10, 'The engine filters rows with time >= startDate and time <= endDate. Fit Ekene-1 from 2020-01-01 with the end date at 2023-01-01 instead of 2022-12-01. What do you get?', '["The same qi 120.000000000000, Di 0.00120000000000000 and R2 1.00000000000000, with a window statement that is now false", "A Hyperbolic fit at b 0.05 and R2 0.999812532094246, the flood-start row lying off the primary curve", "An error, because the end date is exclusive and therefore selects no rows beyond the primary window", "A slightly lower qi and a slightly lower Di, one row of flood-supported production flattening the regression"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both ends of the filter are inclusive, and the flood-start row still sits exactly on the primary decline because the response model freezes the base rate and the lag has not expired. The arithmetic forgives the extra row. The window statement does not, because it now claims primary depletion was the only active drive on the day injection began.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-fit-windows', 11, 'Ekene-1''s primary window books 91666.6666666667 stb at a 10 stb/d limit, and a window starting 2021-01-01 books 56121.8523033298 stb. A colleague says the later window destroyed value. What settles the argument?', '["Refitting the later window with the exponent constrained to zero, restoring the missing volume", "Rescaling the later fit''s qi from 77.3462227639957 back to 120 stb/d, as type-curve normalisation does", "Comparing the two RMSE values, both at the level of double precision", "Adding back the cumulative already produced by that date, Np(366) = 35544.8143633369 stb, which recovers the whole-life EUR exactly"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The later window books only the volume still to come after its start date. Add the cumulative produced by 2021-01-01, 366 days into the well''s life, and the two figures close on the whole-life EUR to within about 4e-11 stb. If the sum does not close, the two fits genuinely disagree; if it closes, they are answering different questions.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-fit-windows', 12, 'Ekene-5 has a response lag of 9 months and the flood ramp lasts 6 months. On what date does its post-ramp exponential regime begin?', '["2024-01-01, the first year boundary lying clear of the flood start", "2024-04-01", "2023-10-01, nine months after injection, when the lag expires and the rate first moves above base", "2023-11-01, the first month recording a rate above the frozen base of 41.3907284768212 stb/d"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Lag plus ramp is 15 months from the 2023-01-01 flood start. From that date the well is a clean exponential at the flood decline constant, starting from base times lift, which is 41.3907284768212 * 1.15 = 47.5993377483444 stb/d, and the fixture''s 2024-04-01 row confirms it. The lag expiring and the ramp completing are two different dates.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-fit-windows', 13, 'Ekene-5 fitted Exponential from 2024-01-01 to 2025-12-01 returns Di 0.000288860363611005 per day at R2 0.889141114126678. Why is the decline constant wrong?', '["The window is too short at 24 rows for the exponential linearisation to resolve a constant this small", "Water cut is climbing over that period, so the oil stream declines faster than the gross does", "The window still admits three rising ramp rows into a falling model, so the fit flattens the decline", "The engine''s t = 0 sits at 2024-01-01 rather than at the flood start, so the constant is measured on the wrong clock"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ekene-5''s ramp does not complete until 2024-04-01, so a January start admits three rising rows. The reported constant comes out 17.4684675397127 percent below the designed post-ramp constant. Ekene-5 never breaks through to water, so water cut plays no part, and the t = 0 convention shifts qi rather than Di.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-fit-windows', 14, 'Ekene-5 fitted Exponential from 2024-02-01 returns R2 0.953962147180947, which getFitQuality reports as Excellent. What is its decline constant doing?', '["It has recovered 0.000350000000000000 per day, which is why the tier flipped to Excellent here", "It reads 0.000189859517828727 per day, the value a 2023-10-01 start produces while the well is still flat at base", "It reads 0.00102764413565850 per day, roughly three times the truth, the oil stream being fitted through a rising water cut", "It reads 0.000315990703997829 per day, still 9.71694171490599 percent below the designed post-ramp constant"]'::jsonb, 3, 'One contaminated month is enough to leave the constant almost ten percent wrong while the quality tier certifies the fit. The tier measures how well one curve tracks the rows it was given and has no way to know those rows span two regimes. The 0.00102764413565850 figure belongs to Ekene-6''s post-breakthrough oil fit.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-fit-windows', 15, 'Booked at a 10 stb/d limit, Ekene-5 returns 130531.301556460 stb from a window starting 2024-01-01 and 106006.169873827 stb from one starting 2024-05-01. What is the four month boundary move worth?', '["24525.1316826323 stb, or 23.1355700444824 percent", "5153.48684678299 stb, about 3.4 percent, the same order as missing the exponent by one grid rung", "Nothing in volume terms, both windows returning the same constant and differing only in reported qi", "About 981 days of well life, volume being fixed by the reservoir and therefore the wrong unit here"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The earlier window still contains ramp, so it books a flatter decline and a longer life. Time to the limit moves as well, from 5409.04191011611 days to 4427.81072941896 days, nearly three years. A boundary choice that feels like a formatting decision is worth almost a quarter of the booking.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-forecast-mechanics', 1, 'generateForecast steps day = 1, 2, 3 and evaluates the model rate at each step. For Ekene-1 at qi 120 stb/d and Di 0.0012 per day, what rate appears on the first row of the returned table?', '["120.000000000000 stb/d, since the table opens at the fitted initial rate before any decline has been applied", "119.856086365450 stb/d, the model rate the loop evaluates at day 1", "119.928028791361 stb/d, which is the exact volume the well delivers during the first day", "10.0090706091916 stb/d"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The loop starts at day = 1, so qi itself never appears as a row and the first entry is 120 times exp(-0.0012). The rejected values are the fitted initial rate, the exact cumulative over the first day, and the rate on the last day the loop produces before the 10 stb/d limit stops it.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-forecast-mechanics', 2, 'Forecasting Ekene-1 to a 10 stb/d limit with stop-at-limit on returns timeToLimit 2071. How many rows does the rates array hold, and why?', '["2071 rows, because the loop pushes a row for every day it evaluates, including the day that trips the limit", "2071 rows, one for each day up to and including the reported timeToLimit", "2070 rows, because the limit check runs before the accumulate step", "2070 rows, because the loop counts from day 0"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The order inside the loop is check, then accumulate, then push. Day 2071 is computed at 9.99706692810967 stb/d, falls below the limit, sets timeToLimit and breaks without contributing anything, so the last produced day is 2070 at 10.0090706091916 stb/d and the array holds 2070 rows.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-forecast-mechanics', 3, 'A forecast is run for 3650 days with stopAtLimit switched off, and the returned timeToLimit reads 3650. What does that number mean?', '["The rate reached 1.50304303452893 stb/d, which the engine treats as an abandonment rate when no limit is supplied", "The horizon was reached without a break, so the field returns the requested duration", "The rate crossed the economic limit on day 3650", "The run stopped once the cumulative reached 98688.2275091051 stb"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When no break occurs, timeToLimit falls back to the total days requested, so the field reports a setting rather than a result. The tell is the array length: a genuine limit crossing leaves fewer rows than the horizon. Neither the ten-year rate nor the ten-year cumulative stops the loop.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-forecast-mechanics', 4, 'A fitted parameter row is labelled Hyperbolic but carries b exactly 0. How does generateForecast evaluate it?', '["On the hyperbolic branch, dividing by (1 + b Di t) raised to 1/b, which is a division by zero that halts the run", "On the exponential branch, because the b test is applied before the model label", "On the harmonic branch, as the degenerate qi/(1 + Di t) case", "It refuses to run and returns an empty rates array"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The branch condition reads modelType Exponential OR b equal to 0, so a zero exponent routes to the exponential rate equation whatever the label says. This is correct behaviour, and it is worth knowing before spending an afternoon debugging a mismatch between a label and a curve.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-forecast-mechanics', 5, 'Ekene-1''s engine daily sum falls short of its closed-form EUR at the same limit. That shortfall is the sum of exactly two separate effects. Which two?', '["Right-endpoint rectangles under a falling curve, plus the rounding of the fitted Di to 0.0012 per day inside the loop", "Floating point drift over 2070 additions", "Left-endpoint rectangles sitting above a falling curve, plus the discarded fractional day", "Right-endpoint rectangles under a falling curve, plus the fractional day discarded at the limit"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The rectangles are read at the end of each day and therefore sit under a decreasing curve, and the sum stops at the last whole produced day while the closed form runs on to the fractional crossing. Left-endpoint rectangles would overstate, the fitted Di is exact to machine precision here, and accumulated float error is many orders of magnitude too small to matter.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-forecast-mechanics', 6, 'Split Ekene-1''s -62.5433065957332 stb shortfall using the exact cumulative at the last produced day, Np(2070) = 91659.1078256737 stb. What are the two terms?', '["-54.9844656027271 stb of discretization and -7.55884099300602 stb of truncation", "-7.55884099300602 stb of discretization, the rest truncation", "-54.9954646954042 and -7.56226812158653 stb, the measured pair", "-59.2366287874611 stb and the remainder"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Subtracting the daily sum from Np(2070) isolates the rectangles at -54.9844656027271 stb, and subtracting the closed-form EUR from Np(2070) isolates the discarded fraction of a day at -7.55884099300602 stb. The values 54.9954646954042 and 7.56226812158653 are the hand predictions of those terms rather than measurements, and -59.2366287874611 stb belongs to the ten-year no-limit run.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-forecast-mechanics', 7, 'For a no-limit exponential forecast the daily right-endpoint sum divided by the exact cumulative is Di exp(-Di) / (1 - exp(-Di)), which is 0.999400120000009 for Ekene-1. What does that ratio not depend on?', '["The nominal decline Di, which cancels along with qi when the two expressions are divided", "The day step, since the sum and the integral both run over whole days", "The horizon and the initial rate", "The economic limit and the decline Di"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both qi and the factor (1 - exp(-Di N)) appear in the sum and in the cumulative, so both cancel and the ratio contains only Di times the step. That is why the engine''s ten-year run comes back 0.0599880000004327 percent low, matching a one-step prediction of 0.0599879999991226 percent without any summation at all.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-forecast-mechanics', 8, 'Ekene-3''s engine forecast returns 111190.357687804 stb against a closed-form 111270.166537926 stb, and its truncation term of -9.83595941342355 stb is larger than Ekene-1''s -7.55884099300602 stb. Why?', '["Its crossing at 2872.98334620742 days falls near the end of a day, so nearly a whole day is discarded", "Its b of 0.5 flattens the tail, so each discarded day carries more volume", "Its initial rate of 150 stb/d lifts every rectangle in the sum", "Its forecast holds 2872 rows against Ekene-1''s 2070"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The truncation term is the volume between the last produced whole day and the exact crossing. Ekene-3 crosses at 2872.98 days, so almost a full day at about 10 stb/d is left out, while Ekene-1 crosses at 2070.75554149000 days and leaves out roughly three quarters of a day.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-forecast-mechanics', 9, 'For an exponential decline held at its left-edge rate over an interval, the overstatement is Di dt / (1 - exp(-Di dt)) - 1. What does that factor depend on?', '["The product of Di and the interval, and also the well''s age, since later intervals sit lower on the curve", "The initial rate and the interval, so a 120 stb/d well overstates more than a 90 stb/d well does", "The cumulative produced so far, which is why the error builds across 36 months", "Only the product of Di and the reporting interval"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The factor exp(-Di t0) appears in both the rectangle and the exact volume for that interval, so it cancels and nothing about qi or the well''s age survives. Every month of the record is overstated by the same percentage, which is why the errors accumulate with one sign instead of averaging away.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-forecast-mechanics', 10, 'Ekene-1 is reported quarterly instead of monthly, so each rate is held for 91 days. What overstatement does the interval law predict?', '["1.87153173403483 percent, the 31 day value, because the law is stated per calendar month", "23.4936115538863 percent, since quarterly rows are annualised before they are summed", "5.55935225601683 percent, about three times the monthly figure", "0.0600120000008753 percent"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Putting Di dt = 0.0012 times 91 into the law returns 5.55935225601683 percent. The rejected values are the 31 day, 365 day and one day results of the same law, each evaluated at the wrong interval.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-forecast-mechanics', 11, 'A database supplies monthly rates. Averaging each month''s opening rate with the next month''s opening rate before multiplying by the days gives what result over Ekene-1''s 36 month primary window?', '["74502.9269694921 stb, the same as holding each opening rate flat for its month", "73157.9366256283 stb exactly", "73166.0847151042 stb, high by 0.0111376698848531 percent", "91604.1233600709 stb"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The trapezoid averages a rectangle that is too high with one that is too low, leaving 8.14808947592974 stb of error against the exact 73157.9366256283 stb. It is about 165 times better than the snapshot for one extra spreadsheet column, and it is still not exact, because the average of two endpoints of a curved function is not that function''s mean value.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-forecast-mechanics', 12, 'An analyst books Ekene-1 as snapshot history to the flood start plus the exact closed-form remainder of 18508.7300410384 stb down to the limit. What is the total, and where does the error sit?', '["93011.6570105305 stb, with all 1344.99034386381 stb of the error carried in by the history leg", "91666.6666666667 stb, because the exact forward leg cancels the overstatement", "93011.6570105305 stb, with the error split evenly between the two legs", "91604.1233600709 stb, the engine daily sum over the whole life"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The forward leg is exact and stays exact, so the composite is over by exactly the amount the snapshot invented, which is 1.46726219330597 percent of the true booking. Two integration conventions meet at a date rather than at a labelled method, and that is what makes this pattern so hard to spot in a finished table.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-forecast-mechanics', 13, 'Why does the Ekene material balance history carry cumulative production from the exact integral, 261475.039999678 stb at 2023-01-01, rather than from monthly snapshots?', '["Snapshot cumulatives cannot be built before the flood start, because the injectors alter the rate within each month", "Material balance works in reservoir barrels while a snapshot sum is in stock tank barrels, so the units would not match", "Material balance reads Np as measured withdrawal, so an inflated Np biases the oil in place it infers", "The engine daily sum is unavailable at field level"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Material balance infers reservoir energy from the volume withdrawn at an observed pressure. A cumulative that is high by 1.8 percent makes the reservoir appear to have supported more withdrawal than it did, and the inferred volume in place moves with it. A snapshot-built cumulative that is handed on must be declared in the same sentence.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-forecast-mechanics', 14, 'Why not keep the engine''s daily sum and divide it by 0.999400120000009 to recover the closed form?', '["The ratio holds only for wells whose economic limit is 10 stb/d, and rescales incorrectly at 5 or 20 stb/d", "The ratio ignores the discarded fractional day, 7.55884099300602 stb here, and it shifts with b", "The ratio would over-correct, since the engine sum already runs high", "The ratio is derived from the monthly snapshot law"]'::jsonb, 1, 'That ratio is exact only for an exponential run with no limit. Switch stop-at-limit on and a discarded fractional day appears that the factor does not model, and move to a hyperbolic well and the ratio depends on the exponent as well. A correction that is exact in one configuration and approximate elsewhere hides which method produced the published number.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-forecast-mechanics', 15, 'A booking line states the model, the fitted parameters, the window, the economic limit and the clock origin. Which convention is still missing, and is the one that separates 91666.6666666667 stb from 91604.1233600709 stb?', '["The forecast horizon in days, without which the sum has no stopping point", "The fit quality tier, without which a reader cannot judge the parameters", "The nominal to effective conversion for the decline", "The integration method"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The two figures come from the same model, the same parameters, the same window and the same limit. Only the integration convention differs, one being the closed-form integral and the other a right-endpoint daily sum, so naming it is what lets a reader using different software reproduce the booking.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-type-curves', 1, 'normalizeByTime writes t = 0 for one point in each well. Where does that origin come from?', '["The earliest first-production date on the field, 2020-01-01, so that every well shares one calendar clock", "The date of the peak rate, so that the normalized curve begins at 1.000 by construction", "The first valid row of the array it is handed, once that array has been sorted by date", "The first day of the fit window"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The function filters out rows with no date or a non-positive rate, sorts what survives, and takes the first surviving date as the origin. That is why Ekene-3 runs from t = 0 at 2020-03-01 and Ekene-6 from t = 0 at 2020-09-01, and why the 184 day calendar offset between them disappears.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-type-curves', 2, 'normalizeByRate divides every rate by a single number. Which one?', '["The fitted qi from that well''s own Arps fit, which is 89.9999999999999 stb/d for Ekene-6", "The maximum rate found in the array it was handed", "The first rate in the array", "The mean rate over the window"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The divisor is the peak of whatever array the function receives, and it is recorded on every row as peakRate. On the noise-free fixture the peak happens to coincide with the first point and with the fitted qi, which is exactly the coincidence that hides the ramp problem on real wells.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-type-curves', 3, 'An engineer concatenates the raw rows of Ekene-3 and Ekene-6 and calls normalizeByTimeAndRate once on the combined array. What does that produce?', '["One global peak of 150 stb/d and one origin of 2020-03-01, so Ekene-6 starts at 0.600000000000000 and at t = 184 days", "A pooled b of 0.05 at R2 0.861590575359367, identical to normalizing each well first", "A failed fit, because the two wells'' dates interleave after sorting", "Identical results, since normalization is applied point by point"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both passes act on the array they receive, so the combined array gets one peak and one origin. Ekene-6 is turned into a well that started six months late and never reached its own peak, and the resulting fit reports b 0.9000000000000002 at R2 0.981509708835585, a higher R2 from a worse procedure. Normalize each well, then pool.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-type-curves', 4, 'Ekene-6''s primary window is normalized by time and rate and then fitted on its own. What comes back?', '["qi_norm 1.00000000000000 with Di and b replaced by the pooled 0.00131674836694260 and 0.05", "qi_norm 1.00000000000000 with b pulled toward 0, because every rate now lies between 0 and 1", "qi_norm 1.00000000000000 with Di and b unchanged at 0.001 per day and 0.35", "A refusal to fit, since the rates are no longer in stb/d"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dividing every rate by a constant and starting the clock at a well''s own first point cannot change the shape parameters. That is the property normalization is used for, and it is also why two normalized wells still disagree wherever their declines genuinely differ.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-type-curves', 5, 'Ekene-3 carries a planted b of 0.5 and Ekene-6 a planted b of 0.35. What b does the pooled type curve return?', '["0.425, the midpoint of the two planted exponents, weighted by each well''s point count", "0.05, below both members and on the bottom rung of the engine''s b grid", "0.35, matching the flatter of the two members", "0.5, matching the better represented member"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The pooled fit returns b 0.05, which is not a compromise between the members and is not an estimate of anything either well does. It is the exponent of the best single curve through a cloud that is not an Arps curve at all, and it sits on the boundary of the search grid, which is itself a warning.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-type-curves', 6, 'The pooled Ekene-3 plus Ekene-6 type curve reports R2 0.861590575359367 over all 62 points. What does the same curve score against Ekene-6''s points alone?', '["0.861590575359367 against each member, since a pooled statistic applies to every contributor", "0.999047938405246, the score the same curve earns once it has been applied to Ekene-6", "0.909718698071357, the better of the two per-member scores", "0.615735522363384"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Scored member by member the pooled curve returns 0.909718698071357 on Ekene-3 and 0.615735522363384 on Ekene-6, so the headline figure is a blend that conceals a poor fit on one of the two wells it claims to represent. The 0.999 value belongs to the later fixed-b application, which is a different fit.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-type-curves', 7, 'Ekene-3 supplies 34 of the pooled cloud''s 62 points and its window runs to 1005 days against Ekene-6''s 821. Why does that matter for a booking?', '["Both wells contribute beyond 821 days, but Ekene-6''s points carry more weight there because there are fewer of them", "All 7 pooled points beyond t = 821 days come from Ekene-3, so it alone shapes the far end", "The pooled curve is undefined beyond 821 days", "Ekene-6 supplies the longer record"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The far end of a pooled type curve is written by whichever wells lived longest, and the far end is where an extrapolated booking spends its barrels. Unequal record lengths inside a pool are therefore a modelling decision, not a data-handling detail.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-type-curves', 8, 'The pooled fit''s R2 of 0.861590575359367 is passed to both quality helpers in the engine. What comes back?', '["Good from getFitQuality and Excellent from calculateTypeCurveQuality, since both bands open at 0.85", "Excellent from both, because R2 above 0.85 with more than 20 points clears every band in the engine", "Fair from getFitQuality and Good from calculateTypeCurveQuality", "Poor from both, since 0.86 fails a type curve"]'::jsonb, 2, 'getFitQuality bands R2 at 0.95, 0.90 and 0.80, so 0.8616 lands on Fair. calculateTypeCurveQuality asks only for R2 above 0.85 with more than 20 points, so the same number reads Good. Two shipped functions, one fit, two labels: read the R2 and the per-member residuals, not the word.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-type-curves', 9, 'What qualifies a group of wells to be pooled into one type curve?', '["Shared decline character, evidenced by each candidate''s own fit clustering in Di and b", "A shared field, operator or licence block, plus a common facility and cost centre", "At least 20 points per well and a pooled R2 above 0.85 on the combined cloud", "A common first-production year and a common producing sand"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Analogy is a physical claim and it has to be evidenced before the pool is built, by fitting every candidate alone and tabulating the results. On the Ekene field that screen returns four wells spanning b from 0 to 1 with Di over a factor of two, so no two of them qualify however much they share administratively.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-type-curves', 10, 'An engineer argues that adding more wells always improves a type curve. What is wrong with that?', '["Nothing is wrong: more points constrain the grid search and narrow the confidence interval on b", "Adding a non-analog raises the point count and the modelling error at the same time", "Adding wells is blocked once the pool passes 60 points", "Adding wells drives R2 to 0.917095895124882"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Pooling all four Ekene producers returns b 0.05 at R2 0.917095895124882, a figure that would pass a casual review while describing none of the four wells. Statistical comfort and modelling error rise together whenever the added wells are not analogous.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-type-curves', 11, 'applyTypeCurve is handed a fitted type curve and a target well''s history. Which of the type curve''s parameters does it use?', '["All three, with the pooled qi_norm of 1.00042521426751 multiplied by the target''s peak rate", "b and Di, with qi rescaled by the target well''s own peak rate of 90 stb/d", "b and qi, with Di taken from the target''s last twelve months", "Only b; qi and Di are refitted on the target"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The pooled qi_norm and Di are discarded. With b held fixed the hyperbolic becomes linear in q raised to the power -b against t, so an ordinary least-squares line on the target''s own rows yields qi from the intercept and Di from the slope. Any method that carries the pooled qi or Di across is a different method and should say so.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-type-curves', 12, 'Ekene-6''s own fit gives qi 90 stb/d and Di 0.001 per day. What do qi and Di become when b is forced down to 0.05?', '["Both rise, since a smaller b has to be offset by a steeper decline and a higher initial rate to hold the history", "qi rises to 90.6896727039003 stb/d while Di falls, which is the pattern a borrowed b above the truth produces", "Both fall, to qi 88.8116671130696 stb/d and Di 0.000893152170857701 per day", "Neither moves; only b was changed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A smaller b makes the decline stiffen instead of softening, so the regression lowers Di to keep the early history from falling away too fast and trims qi to balance the first months. Both free parameters are compensators for the borrowed exponent, which means neither is an independent estimate of anything.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-type-curves', 13, 'The fixed-b match on Ekene-6 returns qi 88.8116671130696 stb/d, Di 0.000893152170857701 per day and b 0.05. What EUR does the hyperbolic closed form give at a 10 stb/d limit?', '["105266.626461929 stb, because the closed form uses the well''s own b of 0.35 whatever the match returned", "91524.2759502962 stb, from a leading factor of 1474.94028511307 times a bracket of 62.0528687663309", "97865.1994120820 stb", "91666.6666666667 stb"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The applied match is a hyperbolic with b 0.05, so the same closed form applies: qi raised to b over Di times (1 - b), multiplied by the difference of qi and the limit each raised to (1 - b). The rejected values are Ekene-6''s true booking, the same match evaluated at a 5 stb/d limit, and Ekene-1''s exponential EUR.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-type-curves', 14, 'The fixed-b match reports R2 0.999047938405246 over history yet under-books Ekene-6 by 13742.3505116328 stb. What explains both facts at once?', '["The borrowed b governs the tail, which lies beyond the 821 days of history over which the R2 was computed", "The 28 monthly points are too few for a stable regression", "The linearization in q raised to -b is biased at low b", "The applied Di of 0.000893152170857701 per day is a typing error"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Summed over the history window the two curves differ by only 0.0352396013199696 percent, so the fit really is excellent where the data is. The data covers 24.8214239567890 percent of the booked life, and the remaining three quarters is produced entirely by the borrowed exponent, which is why the booking is 13.0548028121744 percent low.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-type-curves', 15, 'Ekene-6 is refitted at fixed b values of 0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.35 and 0.5. What do the R2 and EUR columns do?', '["R2 drops below 0.9 away from the true exponent, which identifies b 0.35 without any need for the truth", "EUR stays within 600 stb of 105266.626461929 stb, so the exponent hardly matters at this limit", "R2 and EUR move together, so the best-fitting exponent is also the most conservative booking", "R2 stays above 0.999 throughout while EUR spans more than 20000 stb"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every one of those fits reports R2 above 0.999 and a quality label of Good, while the bookings run from 91524.2759502962 stb to 113989.490502181 stb. On this noise-free fixture R2 does peak at the true exponent, but it peaks in the fifth decimal place while EUR moves by 20 percent, and realistic scatter erases the peak while leaving the spread untouched.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-portfolio-rollups', 1, 'A group roll-up returns a total EUR for four wells. What does rollupGroup actually read to build that total?', '["Each well''s most recently saved scenario for the chosen stream", "The raw monthly rate table for each well, which it refits with auto-select before totalling the four EURs it obtains", "Every scenario saved against the well, averaged", "The group''s combined rate series"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The roll-up never sees production data and never fits anything. Its inputs are a group, a well map, the saved scenario list and one stream, and it takes one scenario per well: the newest whose stream matches.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-portfolio-rollups', 2, 'Two oil scenarios exist for Ekene-3: one saved in March from a defended primary-window fit, one saved in June as a quick test of a full-history fit. Which enters the roll-up?', '["The March scenario, because the roll-up prefers the scenario whose fit reports the higher R2 over its own window", "The June scenario, because selection is by newest createdAt", "Both, with their EURs averaged so the group total does not swing on one analyst''s window", "Neither, until a human resolves it"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Selection is purely by date. Recency is not quality, not relevance and not defensibility, which is why a throwaway scenario saved later can quietly replace considered work in every group that contains the well.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-portfolio-rollups', 3, 'Four wells each carry one saved oil scenario. Someone rolls the same group up for the gas stream. What comes back?', '["A total EUR of 0, with all four wells listed in missingWells", "The same total as the oil roll-up, 461475.535264973 stb, because the stream filter applies only to the combined rate series", "A null result, since no scenario matches", "A total EUR of 0 and an empty missing list"]'::jsonb, 0, 'No scenario carries stream gas, so every well falls through to the missing list, the per-well list is empty and the total is zero. A zero with four names beside it is a coverage problem; a zero read alone is a false statement about the field.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-portfolio-rollups', 4, 'Which roll-up failure mode leaves no trace at all in either totalEur or missingWells?', '["A well in the group with no scenario for the requested stream, whose contribution the total silently absorbs as a zero", "A scenario forecast without stop-at-limit, which the engine flags in the per-well list", "A group whose wells started production in different years", "A throwaway scenario saved after a considered one, which supersedes it on date"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Staleness is invisible in the totals. The only evidence sits in the per-well list, which carries each contributing scenario''s name and createdAt stamp, so reading that list is the check.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-portfolio-rollups', 5, 'The Ekene roll-up of the four base oil scenarios returns a totalEur of 461475.535264973 stb. What is that number?', '["The sum of the four closed-form EURs at the 10 stb/d limit, 461709.132532792 stb, after the engine rounds each one", "A single Arps EUR fitted to the combined monthly rate series of all four wells against a 10 stb/d field-wide limit", "The integral of the combined rate series over 203 months", "The sum of the four scenarios'' engine daily-sum EURs"]'::jsonb, 3, 'totalEur is nothing more than the sum of forecastResults.eur over the selected scenarios, and each of those is a day-by-day sum of forecast rates rather than a closed-form integral.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-portfolio-rollups', 6, 'Ekene-1 books 91666.6666666667 stb, Ekene-3 111270.166537926 stb, Ekene-5 153505.672866270 stb and Ekene-6 105266.626461929 stb from the closed forms at a 10 stb/d limit. What is the field total?', '["535469.764892198 stb, which is what these same four closed forms give once the limit is lowered to 5 stb/d", "461709.132532792 stb, the closed-form field total", "461475.535264973 stb, what the same four wells give when each EUR is taken from its saved daily forecast", "372230.076701345 stb, at a 20 stb/d limit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Four values and three additions. This closed-form total is the field figure the Professional capstone grades, and it can be produced with no engine at all.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-portfolio-rollups', 7, 'The roll-up total falls short of the closed-form total by 233.597267818404 stb, which is exactly the sum of the four per-well shortfalls. Why do they add rather than partly cancel?', '["Because each well''s daily sum is biased the same way, low, by construction", "Because the four shortfalls are independent random rounding errors, so they combine as the square root of the sum of their squares", "Because only Ekene-3 carries a shortfall, and 79.8088501215243 stb of it survives into the group total", "Because the limit is applied once at field level"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A right-endpoint sum of a declining rate understates the integral on every step, for every well. One-sided bias accumulates across a portfolio instead of averaging out, and it scales with the number of wells.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-portfolio-rollups', 8, 'A right-endpoint daily sum misses roughly half the total rate drop across a forecast. For Ekene-3, at qi 150 stb/d and a 10 stb/d limit, what does that estimate give against the actual shortfall?', '["About 750 stb, against an actual 79.8088501215243 stb, with the remainder absorbed by the stop-at-limit cutoff", "About 45 stb, matching Ekene-5''s shortfall of 44.9876250025700 stb almost exactly and so applying to Ekene-3 too", "About 70 stb, against an actual 233.597267818404 stb", "About 70 stb, against an actual 79.8088501215243 stb"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Half the drop from 150 stb/d to the 10 stb/d limit is 70 stb, and the true gap is 79.8088501215243 stb, the residue being the truncated final step. The estimate earns its keep as an order of magnitude: tens of barrels per well, never thousands.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-portfolio-rollups', 9, 'What does the rate field of a combinedRates entry hold?', '["The field''s average producing rate for that month, in stb/d", "The month''s produced volume in stb, from the average daily rate times the days in the month", "The arithmetic sum of every daily rate point binned into that month", "The highest daily rate reached that month"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The binning code does cur.rate += pt.rate and nothing else. The value therefore grows with the rates, with the number of days in the month and with the number of contributing wells, and only one of those three is about field performance.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-portfolio-rollups', 10, 'The peak bin, 2020-10, carries a rate value of 10949.2274024767, and October has 31 days. What is the field''s average daily oil rate for that month?', '["353.200883950861 stb/d, dividing the bin by 31 days", "2737.30685061917 stb/d, dividing the bin by the four wells that were on production during October 2020", "88.3002209877153 stb/d, dividing the bin by the 124 that the wells field reports for that month", "10949.2274024767 stb/d, as reported"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Dividing a bin by the number of days it covers turns a sum of daily rates into an average rate. The 88.3 value is the mean rate per contributing well-day, a different quantity that belongs on no field chart.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-portfolio-rollups', 11, 'In October 2020 the wells field reads 124 for a field with four producers. What is it counting?', '["Well-days of production, which a reserves report would summarise for a four-well field as producing well-months", "Forecast points in that month, four wells times 31 days", "Distinct forecast dates across the four scenarios, October''s 31 plus the 93 carried in from the third quarter", "Wells, stored once per day of the month"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The line is cur.wells += 1, executed once per forecast point, so on a daily forecast it counts about 30 per well per month. A four-well field reporting 124 wells is only invisible because nobody reads a field called wells sceptically.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-portfolio-rollups', 12, 'The 2020-01 bin holds 3533.84949361315 with a wells value of 30, while the closed-form volume for the whole of January is 3651.65806057785 stb. Where does the 117.808566964693 stb difference come from?', '["Three of the four producers were not yet on stream that month, so their January volumes are absent from the bin", "The daily sum understates the exact integral by 0.0682290617407998 percent, which accounts for the whole difference", "The forecast''s earliest point is dated 2020-01-02, so 1 January is missing", "The engine bins in fixed 30-day months"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The forecast loop starts at day 1 and adds it to the start date, so the earliest point is dated one day after start-up. The bin holds 30 of January''s 31 days, and the absent day is worth about one day of production near the initial 120 stb/d.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-portfolio-rollups', 13, 'September 2020 reports a wells value of 119. Ekene-6 came on stream 2020-09-01 and the other three producers were already on. Reconstruct that count.', '["Four wells at 30 points, less one discarded because Ekene-6''s initial rate exceeds the facility constraint", "Three wells at 30 points plus Ekene-6''s 29, which begin on 2 September", "119 well-days of uptime, once September downtime has been deducted", "One point per well per day, less the flood day"]'::jsonb, 1, 'September has 30 days, so Ekene-1, Ekene-3 and Ekene-5 contribute 30 points each for 90, and Ekene-6 contributes 29 because its forecast begins on 2 September. Anyone who can do that arithmetic will never plot this field as a well count.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-portfolio-rollups', 14, 'Ekene-5''s scenario is deleted and the same four-well group is rolled up again. The total comes back 308014.850023706 stb. What should a reviewer notice before anything else?', '["That the combined series still runs to 2036-11, which proves Ekene-5''s forecast is still being binned into the months", "That the total is 66.7456509578234 percent of the four-well total", "That the total still sits inside the 2000 stb capstone tolerance", "One entry in missingWells, and 153460.685241267 stb unrepresented"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The total is perfectly formed, correctly signed and in the right order of magnitude for a four-well field, and Ekene-5''s entire booking is absent from it. Only the missing list says so, which is why it gets read before the total.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-portfolio-rollups', 15, 'The four closed forms total 461709.132532792 stb at a 10 stb/d limit, 535469.764892198 stb at 5 stb/d and 372230.076701345 stb at 20 stb/d. What does that 163239.688190854 stb swing show?', '["That the harmonic well is mis-modelled, since only a harmonic tail can move a field total by more than 15 percent", "That the four fits are unstable and need refitting", "That the limit is an assumption of the booking, not a property of the reservoir", "That a field EUR without its limit is conservative"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Not one fitted parameter changed across those three totals. A field EUR line that does not carry its economic limit invites a reader to mistake a change of commercial assumption for a change in reservoir understanding.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-booking', 1, 'What separates the Professional booking workflow from the Associate one?', '["The economic limit moves from a per-well rate to a field abandonment rate applied once to the roll-up total", "The window, the model family and any analogy stop being given and become decisions you defend", "Closed forms give way to daily forecast sums", "RMSE replaces R2 as the statistic that picks the family"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The calculations are the same ones. What is added is a decision at each step and a written defence of it, which is why the workflow grows a regime log, an analogy ruling and a stated forecast convention.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-booking', 2, 'Why does the workflow insist the regime log is written before any fit is run?', '["Because auto-select requires the event dates", "Because a log written afterwards is the log that justifies the fit", "Because the server-side grader rejects a submission whose window boundaries were not logged in advance", "Because boundaries resolved to the month need timestamps that are only available before fitting begins"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The order is a safeguard against your own hindsight. Every entry in the log is a candidate window boundary, and a log assembled after seeing the fits cannot be distinguished, even by its author, from a rationalisation.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-booking', 3, 'A full-history fit of Ekene-1 returns qi 97.2058663778433 stb/d on a well whose first recorded rate is 120 stb/d. What does that say?', '["That qi is a fitted parameter rather than a measurement, so agreement with the earliest reading is not expected", "That the fit is correctly discounting an unstable flush period, which is why qi sits below the earliest reading", "That the curve cannot reach the start of the data it was fitted to", "That the well was choked back in its first month"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A fitted qi materially below the well''s own early rates is a structural complaint about the window, not a nuance of parameter estimation. The single curve is being pulled down by rows from a second regime.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-booking', 4, 'A fit returns b sitting exactly on 1.95, the top of the engine''s search range. What is the correct reading?', '["That the grid search has converged, since a parameter landing exactly on a grid value is at a true optimum", "That the well is in transient flow, where b between 1.5 and 2 is expected and bookable", "That b is unidentifiable and should be reported as 1.95 with a note", "That the optimizer wanted a larger b and was stopped by the range"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A parameter pinned on a search boundary is an alarm rather than a result. It says the best curve the model can offer lies outside the range you allowed, which almost always means the rows span more than one regime.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-booking', 5, 'Forcing the wrong family on Ekene-3''s primary window gives exponential R2 0.987334417750128 and harmonic R2 0.951593684935824, when the truth is hyperbolic with b of 0.5. What follows?', '["The exponential fit should be preferred, since 0.987334417750128 is the highest R2 available on that window", "Both clear the Excellent band, so R2 alone cannot choose the family", "The harmonic fit is Good rather than Excellent, so the quality bands do separate the two after all", "The true b is the average of the two"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both sit above the 0.95 threshold the quality bands call Excellent. Over a short early window the three families are nearly indistinguishable by fit statistic, which is why the family is argued from physics and window length.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-booking', 6, 'The workflow requires an explicit ruling on analogy. When does that ruling have to be recorded?', '["Only when the well has no history of its own", "Only when wells from more than one field are pooled, a shared field name being sufficient evidence within a field", "Always, including when you decide not to use one", "Only when the pooled fit''s R2 falls below 0.95"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A decision not to borrow is as auditable as a decision to borrow, and it is the one a reviewer cannot reconstruct from the output. Pooling Ekene-3 with Ekene-6 returns b of 0.05 at R2 0.861590575359367, matching neither member, which is exactly the kind of ruling worth recording.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-booking', 7, 'Why must a booking name which forecast number it reports?', '["Because the closed form is exact and the daily sum is an approximation that has no place in a report", "Because the two conventions differ by more than any reserves audit would tolerate on a field total", "Because the capstone grader accepts closed-form values only", "Because both are legitimate, they differ, and the next engineer has to land on your figure rather than near it"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The closed form integrates and the engine''s forecast sums daily rates, so on a declining well the forecast sum lands slightly lower. Naming the convention is what makes the figure reproducible.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-booking', 8, 'At the roll-up step of the Professional workflow, what gets read before the total?', '["The count of monthly bins, a short combined series being the signature of a well whose scenario is absent", "missingWells, then the per-well scenario names and dates", "The combined rate series and its peak month", "The total against the closed-form sum, since agreement confirms every scenario in the group is current"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Coverage before provenance, and provenance before arithmetic. A total can be well formed and still be missing a well or built on a stale scenario, and neither of those shows up in the total itself.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-booking', 9, 'The capstone grades Ekene-3''s b to a tolerance of 0.02 while the engine steps b through its grid in increments of 0.05. What does that combination mean in practice?', '["That any b between 0.45 and 0.55 passes, the grid being unable to resolve more finely than one step", "That the raw float 0.49999999999999994 fails unless it is rounded to 0.5 before it is entered", "That a fit landing one grid step from the truth fails the field", "That both neighbouring grid values pass"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The tolerance is 2.5 times smaller than the step, not larger, so the nearest wrong grid values of 0.45 and 0.55 are both outside it. The raw float is far inside the tolerance and needs no rounding.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-booking', 10, 'In the hyperbolic EUR formula applied to Ekene-3, with qi 150 stb/d, Di 0.002 per day and b 0.5, what is the prefactor qi raised to b, divided by (1-b) times Di?', '["12.2474487139159, the square root of 150, before it is divided by (1-b) times Di", "9.08517105374751, the bracket", "111270.166537926, the value once the bracket has already been applied", "12247.4487139159, which is 12.2474487139159 divided by 0.001"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The square root of 150 is 12.2474487139159 and (1-b) times Di is 0.001, so the prefactor is 12247.4487139159. Multiplying it by the bracket 9.08517105374751 gives the booked EUR.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-booking', 11, 'The capstone asks for Ekene-6''s EUR from the fixed-b type curve. The panel shows 91524.2759502962 stb from the match and 105266.626461929 stb as the well''s own closed-form EUR. Which is graded?', '["The type-curve value, because the field asks what the method produced", "The closed-form value, because a capstone would not grade a number known to be 13.0548028121742 percent low", "Either one, the 600 stb tolerance admits both", "Their average, which is how a borrowed-curve booking is reconciled"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The graded field names the type-curve route, and the tolerance of 600 stb is far narrower than the 13742.3505116328 stb between the two. Reporting what a method produced while knowing it under-books is the exact situation a borrowed curve creates on a well with no history.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-booking', 12, 'One graded field asks for the R2 of Ekene-1''s full-history fit, a fit this tier teaches you to reject. What is that testing?', '["That the full-history window is admissible after all, provided its R2 of 0.818388421218434 is quoted with the parameters", "That R2 drops below 0.90 whenever a window spans two regimes, which is the operational test for an invalid window", "That you can report a rejected fit''s statistic without booking the fit", "That a b of 1.95 is bookable once noted"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Reporting a diagnostic is not endorsing it. The naive fit''s R2 is evidence in a rejection memo, and quoting it accurately is part of showing the reviewer what you rejected and why.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-booking', 13, 'Of the five parts of a window justification, which is the one most often missing from real notes and the one a reviewer most wants?', '["The counterfactual: what the rejected alternative window gives", "The physical basis, which names the event fixing each boundary", "The boundary convention, which states how the event date was resolved against monthly rows", "The residual risk, which states what evidence would reopen the decision"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Without the alternative and its result, a reviewer who disagrees has to redo the work before the disagreement can even be stated. Running the rejected window and recording what it gave is what makes a note auditable.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-booking', 14, 'Why is ''the window with the best R2'' a dangerous selection rule on real, scattered data?', '["Because R2 rises with the number of points, so the rule always selects the full history including the flood response", "Because R2 is undefined for hyperbolic fits, which leaves the rule inapplicable to any well with b above zero", "Because the bands call anything above 0.80 Fair, so R2 cannot discriminate between candidate windows at all", "Because shorter windows fit better, and short windows leave b unidentified"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Fewer points are easier to pass a curve through, so a fit-quality selection rule drifts toward short windows, and a short window is precisely where the decline exponent is least constrained. The evidence for a boundary is an event, not a residual.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-booking', 15, 'Ekene-6 refitted from 2024-01-01 onward returns Exponential with Di 0.00114387174233099 per day at R2 0.920390851618676. Why is the oil decline steeper than the flood''s own decline constant?', '["Because an exponential family always reports a steeper Di than the hyperbolic it replaces over the same rows", "Because water cut is climbing, so the oil stream falls faster than total fluid", "Because the window still contains the tail of the injection ramp, which biases the fitted Di upward", "Because R2 0.920390851618676 is a poor fit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The oil stream and the gross fluid stream decline differently once water breaks through. It is the reason a window statement has to name the stream: you fit the stream you book, and you say what drives it.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 1, 'In the Arps family, what does the decline exponent b describe?', '["How fast the decline rate itself declines", "The fraction of the initial rate still flowing after one year, 0.463296166592870 in Ekene-3''s case", "The curvature of the rate-time trace", "The ratio of nominal to effective decline"]'::jsonb, 0, 'b governs the rate of change of the decline rate. At b of 0 the fractional loss per day never changes, and as b rises the decline itself slows with time, which is what fattens the tail.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 2, 'Which b values correspond to the exponential and harmonic forms?', '["b of 1 for exponential and b of 0 for harmonic, the exponent counting the order of the decline", "b of 0 for exponential and b of 1.95 for harmonic, 1.95 being the ceiling of the engine''s search", "b of 0 for exponential and b of 1 for harmonic", "b of 0.5 and b of 1.5"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Exponential is the b of 0 limit and harmonic is the b of 1 case. Everything strictly between them is hyperbolic, and Ekene-3 at 0.5 and Ekene-6 at 0.35 both sit in that interior.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 3, 'Ekene-3''s b tile reads 0.49999999999999994 rather than 0.5 on a noise-free fixture whose planted b is exactly 0.5. Why?', '["Because the engine stores b in single precision, which cannot represent 0.5", "Because the grid is built by repeated addition of 0.05, and the accumulated float is not the decimal", "Because the optimizer converged marginally below the planted value, the window ending before the flood", "Because the fit is a least-squares compromise between the planted 0.5 and the harmonic bound at 1"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The search steps b by adding 0.05 repeatedly, and binary floating point does not return exactly 0.5 after ten such additions. The value is the accumulated float, and it should be read rather than rounded away.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 4, 'Forcing the wrong family on Ekene-3''s primary window gives exponential RMSE 3.58830309063897 and harmonic RMSE 7.01499799089027, while both R2 values read above 0.95. What does the RMSE pair add?', '["Nothing, since RMSE and R2 are equivalent", "It proves the harmonic fit invalid", "It sets the confidence interval on b, whose half-width is the RMSE divided by the number of points", "It separates two candidates that R2 places in the same quality band"]'::jsonb, 3, 'R2 puts both forced fits in the Excellent band while RMSE, in the units of the rate, is nearly twice as large for the harmonic. Reading the two together is what keeps a plausible R2 from settling the family question.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 5, 'The engine''s fit-quality bands label a fit Excellent, Good, Fair or Poor. What R2 does Excellent require?', '["0.90 and above, with Good starting at 0.80 and Fair at 0.70 for fits made over short windows", "0.99 and above, which is why a type-curve match at 0.999047938405246 is reported as Excellent", "0.95 and above", "0.80 and above, the same threshold a reserves auditor applies to an Arps fit"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The bands run 0.95 and above for Excellent, 0.90 for Good and 0.80 for Fair, anything lower being Poor. They describe agreement with the rows you selected and say nothing about whether those rows were the right ones.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 6, 'Why is b poorly determined over a short early window even on noise-free data?', '["Because the three families barely diverge until enough decline has accumulated", "Because early rates are flush", "Because the grid search cannot start until at least two years of monthly rows are available to it", "Because R2 is undefined until the well has declined to half of its initial rate, leaving b unconstrained"]'::jsonb, 0, 'b is the parameter that describes how the decline changes over time, so it needs observed change in the decline to be identified. Early on, exponential, hyperbolic and harmonic curves through the same points are almost superimposed.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 7, 'Two of the four naive full-history fits, on Ekene-1 and Ekene-3, both return b of exactly 1.95000000000000. What is 1.95?', '["The integrability limit of the model", "The b value the flood response happens to impose on any well fitted across the 2023-01-01 boundary", "The average of the planted exponents across the four Ekene producers, weighted by their EURs", "The top of the engine''s b search range"]'::jsonb, 3, 'It is the search ceiling. Two different wells landing on the same value to fifteen figures is the signature of a boundary rather than of a fitted result, and the correct response is to revisit the window.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 8, 'Ahmed''s Ikoku gas-well example solves the decline exponent as b of 0.5195. What would this engine''s grid search return for the same data?', '["0.5195, the grid refining until it converges", "The nearest 0.05 step, because the grid cannot express 0.5195", "1.95, because a b above 0.5 always drives a grid search to its ceiling on gas-well data", "0.5, because b is reported to one decimal"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A grid search resolves only to its own step. A Newton solver returns a continuous value, so the two methods disagree at the fourth decimal by construction, and the difference is a property of the tool rather than of the reservoir.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 9, 'What is a fit window, in the sense this tier uses the term?', '["A display preference controlling which rows the chart draws, without effect on the fitted parameters", "The interval over which the limit applies", "A statement about which physical regime the well is in", "The span of rows the engine needs before auto-select has enough points to distinguish three families"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Choosing rows is choosing which physics you claim the data represents. That is why the boundary is argued from events rather than from residuals, and why the choice belongs in the booking.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 10, 'Ekene-1 gives R2 1.00000000000000 on its primary window and R2 0.818388421218434 across the full history. What does the pair show?', '["That the full-history fit is merely less precise", "That the primary window is overfitted, R2 of exactly 1 being the signature of too few points", "That R2 always falls as a window lengthens", "That a single Arps curve cannot span both the primary decline and the flood response"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The data is noise-free, so a correctly windowed fit recovers the planted decline exactly. The drop to 0.818388421218434 measures the cost of asking one curve to describe two regimes, not a loss of precision.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 11, 'Ekene-6''s naive full-history fit returns b of 1.35000000000000 at R2 0.780507944377468, while its primary window returns b of 0.35. What is the 1.35 curve doing?', '["Splitting the difference between primary decline and flood response", "Recovering the well''s true post-flood decline exponent, which the primary window cannot see", "Reporting the sum of the primary b and the unit increment the flood adds to any hyperbolic fit", "Fitting the flood response alone"]'::jsonb, 0, 'One curve is being asked to pass through two regimes, and the compromise it reaches is a b that describes neither. A b above 1 also implies a tail this well has produced no evidence for.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 12, 'The Ekene waterflood starts on 2023-01-01 and the rate data is monthly, one reading on the first of each month. Where does a primary window end?', '["On 2023-01-01 itself, since that row is the last one recorded before injection changes the rates", "At the last complete monthly row before the event month", "Halfway through December 2022", "Wherever the residuals show a break"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Resolve the event date to the resolution of the data and say so in the note. The convention matters less than stating it, because a reader who applies a different one will not reproduce your parameters.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 13, 'Ekene-5 refitted from 2024-01-01 onward returns Exponential with Di 0.000288860363611005 per day at R2 0.889141114126678, which does not represent its settled post-response behaviour. Why not?', '["Because an exponential family cannot represent a flooded well, whose response is always hyperbolic", "Because R2 of 0.889141114126678 sits in the Fair band, and Fair fits are never used for booking", "Because 2024-01-01 falls in the middle of a month, so the window starts on a partial monthly row", "Because the flood response ramp on this well runs into 2024, so the window still catches the ramp tail"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Ekene-5''s response lags the injection start, so its ramp only ends in April 2024. A window opened three months too early mixes ramp and settled decline, and moving the start is the difference between describing one regime and averaging two.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 14, 'Ekene-6''s post-response oil decline is steeper than the decline of the total fluid the flood supports. What does that require of a window statement?', '["That it name the stream it applies to", "That it quote the water cut at the start and end of the window, since Di scales with the change between them", "That it use the gross fluid stream, which is the only stream whose decline the flood constant describes", "That it be reopened whenever water cut rises by more than the tolerance stated in the booking"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Oil and gross fluid decline differently once water breaks through, so a window that does not say which stream it describes is ambiguous. You fit the stream you book, and you say what drives it.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 15, 'How does generateForecast build a forecast?', '["It differences the closed-form cumulative", "It integrates the rate equation analytically and reports the result on a monthly grid for charting", "It evaluates the rate at day 1, day 2 and so on, adding each value as a full day of production", "It interpolates from qi down to the limit"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The loop steps whole days from day 1, accumulates the rate at each step, and breaks the first time the rate falls below the economic limit when stop-at-limit is set. Everything the forecast reports follows from that construction.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 16, 'Ekene-1''s engine forecast accumulates 91604.1233600709 stb against a closed-form EUR of 91666.6666666667 stb at the same limit. What is the sign of the difference and why?', '["Higher, because the loop counts day 1 twice, once as the initial rate and once as the first step", "Lower, because a right-endpoint sum of a declining rate understates the integral", "Lower, because the forecast is truncated at 10 years while the closed form runs to the limit", "Higher, because it holds each opening rate"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rate at the end of a day is the lowest rate in that day, so every step credits slightly too little. The shortfall of 62.5433065957332 stb is 0.0682290617407998 percent of the booking.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 17, 'The closed form puts Ekene-1''s 10 stb/d limit at 2070.75554149000 days and the engine reports a time to limit of 2071 days. What is the engine reporting?', '["The first whole day on which the rate is below the limit", "The crossing rounded to a whole day", "The last day on which the rate is still above the limit, after which the forecast is halted", "The number of daily points"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The loop breaks when it first sees a rate below the limit and reports that day, so the value is a step count rather than a rounded crossing. The forecast that day is discarded, which is why the series carries 2070 points.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 18, 'Run Ekene-1 for ten years with no economic limit and the forecast accumulates 98688.2275091051 stb, against a closed-form Np at 3650 days of 98747.4641378926 stb. What does that comparison isolate?', '["The effect of the economic limit, which removes the tail below 10 stb/d from the booking", "The difference between a nominal and an effective decline applied over the same ten years", "The rounding of the forecast duration", "The discretization alone, with no stop-at-limit truncation involved"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Removing the limit removes the truncated final step, so the remaining 59.2366287875047 stb of difference is pure right-endpoint bias. It is the clean way to see that the two effects are separate.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 19, 'Ekene-1''s cumulative to the flood start is 73157.9366256283 stb by the exact integral and 74502.9269694921 stb if each month''s opening rate is held for the whole month. Why does the second overstate?', '["Because monthly snapshots ignore the days lost to downtime, which the integral accounts for", "Because the rate on the first of the month is the highest rate in that month for a declining well", "Because a month is treated as 30 days, adding five extra days a year to the accumulation", "Because the snapshot method compounds the nominal decline monthly rather than continuously"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Holding the opening rate credits barrels never produced, once a month, in the same direction every time. Over three years that one-sided bias reaches 1344.99034386381 stb, which is why it does not average out.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 20, 'Ekene-1''s daily-sum EUR and its closed-form EUR differ by 0.0682290617407998 percent. What does that imply about a capstone EUR field graded to a tolerance of 600 stb?', '["That only the closed-form value passes reliably", "That the daily sum must be corrected upward by 0.0682290617407998 percent before it is submitted", "That the tolerance is not testing your integration method, since either route passes", "That EUR is uncertain at that level anyway"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The gap between the two legitimate routes is far inside the tolerance, so the field is testing the window, the family and the limit instead. It remains your job to know which convention produced the number you entered.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 21, 'What does normalizing wells by time and rate achieve before a type curve is fitted?', '["It removes the exponent from each well", "It converts rates into cumulative volumes", "It rescales every well to the same economic limit so that their EURs can be added directly", "It puts wells with different start dates and initial rates on one common axis pair"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Time is measured from each well''s own start and rate is divided by each well''s own initial rate, so four wells that came on stream in different months can be overlaid as a single cloud.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 22, 'Pooling Ekene-3 and Ekene-6, whose planted exponents are 0.5 and 0.35, returns a type curve with b of 0.05 at R2 0.861590575359367. What has happened?', '["The pooled b is the harmonic mean of the two members, which always lies below the smaller value", "Normalization removed the curvature from both wells", "The single best hyperbolic through a cloud of two different declines is nearly exponential", "The fit hit the bottom of the search range"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The cloud mixes two different Di values as well as two exponents, and the curve that minimises error across the union collapses toward a straight line on semilog axes. The result matches neither member.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 23, 'The pooled Ekene-3 and Ekene-6 type curve reports a normalized qi of 1.00042521426751. Why is that number so close to 1?', '["Because rate normalization divides each well by its own initial rate", "Because both wells were planted with the same initial rate, so their pooled qi is that rate divided by itself", "Because the engine constrains normalized qi to the interval around 1 to keep the pooled fit stable", "Because a normalized qi above 1 is rejected"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every well enters the cloud starting at 1 on the normalized rate axis, so the fitted intercept lands near 1 by construction. The informative outputs of the pooled fit are Di, b and R2.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 24, 'What makes a set of wells admissible as an analogy for a type curve?', '["A shared field name, which is what defines the analogous population in a reserves report", "Shared decline character, drive and completion", "A pooled fit whose R2 clears the 0.95 threshold the quality bands call Excellent for the group", "At least three wells"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Ekene pooling is the counterexample: two wells from the same field, both hyperbolic, and the pooled curve matches neither because their declines differ in character. Membership of a field is not evidence of analogy.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 25, 'Applying the pooled curve''s fixed b of 0.05 to Ekene-6 returns qi 88.8116671130696 stb/d, Di 0.000893152170857701 per day and R2 0.999047938405246. How should that R2 be read?', '["As confirmation that b of 0.05 is Ekene-6''s true exponent, since a wrong b could not fit this well", "As evidence that the pooled curve is better than Ekene-6''s own fit, which cannot exceed R2 of 1", "As agreement with the history, achieved by adjusting qi and Di while b was held", "As the probability the tail is right"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two free parameters are enough to hug an observed decline at almost any fixed b. The high R2 is a statement about the rows that were fitted and carries no information about the extrapolated tail.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 26, 'The fixed-b match books Ekene-6 at 91524.2759502962 stb against its true closed-form EUR of 105266.626461929 stb. What is the lesson of that pairing?', '["That a fit can look excellent over the history and still under-book by 13.0548028121742 percent", "That type curves need three years of history", "That the borrowed b of 0.05 is too low, and any b below the well''s true 0.35 will under-book by about 13 percent", "That the EUR gap of 13742.3505116328 stb is within normal booking uncertainty for a type-curve match"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Goodness of fit over the history and accuracy of the booking are different properties. The borrowed exponent controls the tail, and the tail is where the volume the match omits actually lives.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 27, 'A borrowed curve matches a well''s history at R2 0.999047938405246. What does that R2 tell you about the tail it extrapolates?', '["That the tail is reliable to about the same 0.1 percent as the fitted history, given the fit quality", "That the tail is conservative, since a fit this good cannot overstate a forecast beyond the data", "That the tail carries the uncertainty of the pooled curve, which fitted its own cloud at R2 0.861590575359367", "Nothing at all"]'::jsonb, 3, 'R2 is computed only over the rows that were fitted. The extrapolated tail has no observations in it, so no fit statistic over the history can speak to it, which is precisely why the borrowed b has to be argued rather than measured.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 28, 'A new well has no production history and a type curve is the only route to a booking. What does the booking have to state?', '["The R2 of the pooled fit, which quantifies the risk that the borrowed curve is wrong for this well", "That the exponent is borrowed, from which wells, and on what evidence of analogy", "The number of wells in the pool", "That the EUR is provisional and will be replaced once the well has produced for a full calendar year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The borrowed exponent is the largest assumption in such a booking and the one a reviewer must be able to challenge. Naming the source wells and the analogy argument is what makes the challenge possible.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 29, 'The roll-up''s per-well list carries a scenario name and a createdAt stamp beside each EUR. What are those two fields for?', '["They allow the engine to order the contributions chronologically when it bins the combined rate series", "They record which analyst created the scenario, so that a booking can be attributed in the reserves file", "They let the engine drop duplicate scenarios", "They let a reviewer see which saved work each contribution came from"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Selection is by date alone, so provenance is the only defence against a stale or throwaway scenario silently entering a total. The per-well list is where that provenance is visible.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 30, 'How does the roll-up decide which month a forecast point belongs to?', '["By counting 30-day periods from the earliest start date among the wells in the group", "By day index divided by 30", "From the calendar year and month of the point''s own date", "By aligning every well to a common month index measured from its own first production"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Points are keyed by the UTC year and month of their own date, so wells that came on stream in different months land in the same calendar bins. That is what makes the combined series a field-time series rather than a well-age one.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 31, 'The Ekene roll-up total is 0.0505940323374074 percent below the closed-form total of the same four wells. What kind of quantity is that percentage?', '["A measure of how much the flood response has already reduced the field''s primary bookings", "A numerical artefact of the forecast, roughly constant however many wells the group holds", "The rounding error of the engine''s floating-point arithmetic accumulated over 203 monthly bins", "The share of the field EUR contributed by wells whose scenarios were saved before the flood began"]'::jsonb, 1, 'It is right-endpoint discretization, well by well, expressed as a fraction. Because the bias and the total both scale with the number of wells, the percentage stays about the same as a portfolio grows.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 32, 'The final bin of the Ekene combined series, 2036-11, reports a wells value of 4. What does that mean?', '["Four forecast points fell in that month, from one well", "Four wells were still producing that month, all of them at rates near the 10 stb/d limit", "The series has four bins remaining before the last well reaches its economic limit and stops", "All four scenarios contributed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ekene-5 is the only well whose forecast reaches 2036, and it stops on 2036-11-04. The counter runs low at the end of life for the same reason it runs to 124 at peak: it counts points.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 33, 'The 2020-11 bin of the Ekene combined series reads 10194.8888809911 with all four wells producing for the whole of a 30-day month. What is the field''s average oil rate that month?', '["328.867383257777 stb/d, dividing the bin by the 31 days the engine allows for a standard month", "2548.72222024778 stb/d, dividing the bin by the four wells that produced through the month", "339.829629366370 stb/d", "84.9574073415925 stb/d, dividing the bin by the 120 that the wells field reports for that month"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dividing by the 30 days November covers turns a sum of daily rates into an average field rate. Using the wells value as a divisor gives a mean per contributing well-day, which is a different quantity.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 34, 'Ekene-5 contributes 33.2472680417183 percent of the Ekene closed-form field total, the largest share of the four, despite having the lowest initial rate at 100 stb/d. Why?', '["Because it came on stream latest of the four and so has the longest remaining producing life ahead of it", "Because its nominal decline of 0.0015 per day is the lowest of the four producers", "Because its forecast runs 6000 days, and EUR scales with the length of the forecast that produced it", "Because it is harmonic, and the harmonic tail carries the most volume down to a low limit"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A harmonic decline flattens as it falls, so it takes 6000 days to reach 10 stb/d and accumulates far beyond what its initial rate suggests. Its 0.0015 per day is in fact the second highest nominal decline of the four.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 35, 'Which statement about the Ekene field total of 461709.132532792 stb is correct?', '["It excludes the flood response and is not reconciled against material balance", "It is the field''s reserves, being the sum of four independently fitted and booked wells at a stated limit", "It represents a P50 outcome", "It applies a field abandonment rate rather than a per-well limit, which is why the four EURs can be summed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Four primary-depletion extrapolations at a per-well limit, with no economics, no flood, no volumetric reconciliation and no range. Every one of those exclusions belongs in the line that quotes the number.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 36, 'Of everything that determines a booked EUR, which input leaves no trace whatever in the reported output?', '["The economic limit, which is absorbed into the EUR and cannot be recovered from it afterwards", "The window", "The model family, since auto-select reports only the parameters and not the family it chose", "The forecast convention, which changes the EUR by more than any tolerance a report would apply"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Family, parameters, R2 and RMSE are all printed, and the limit is normally quoted beside the volume. The rows that were fitted are gone by the time anyone reads the booking, which is why the window has to be written down.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 37, 'The capstone grades Ekene-6''s windowed Di of 0.001 per day to a tolerance of 0.00002. What fraction of the graded value is that?', '["0.2 percent, so only a value carried to five decimal places has any chance of passing the field", "20 percent, admitting the full-history Di", "2 percent, which is wide enough to admit any fit whose R2 clears the Excellent band on its window", "2 percent, which is tight enough that a full-history fit fails the field"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The tolerance is 2 percent of 0.001 per day. It is a window test rather than a precision test: fit the correct regime and any honest route lands inside it.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 38, 'You are writing a memo rejecting Ekene-1''s full-history fit. Which evidence carries the argument?', '["Its R2 of 0.818388421218434, which falls below the 0.90 threshold a booking requires", "The absence of a confidence interval on b, without which no fitted exponent can be accepted for booking", "A qi below the well''s own early rates, a b pinned on the search ceiling, and a window spanning two regimes", "Its Di of 0.00196150586036441 per day, which exceeds the planted 0.0012 by more than half"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The fit statistic is a symptom that a reviewer will ask for, not the argument. The argument is that the curve cannot reach its own data, that the optimizer was stopped by a boundary, and that no single Arps curve is the right object across an operational change.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 39, 'Which set of clauses makes a single-well volume into a booking?', '["The volume, the economic limit, the window, the stream, the family and parameters, and the conventions used", "The volume and the economic limit, the rest being working detail that belongs in the analyst''s file", "The volume, the R2 of the fit and the fit quality tier, which together establish that the fit is acceptable", "The volume, the forecast duration in days, and the date on which the scenario was saved to the project"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Anything you leave out, the reader supplies from assumption, and their assumption will not be yours. The test of a booking is whether another engineer can reproduce the number from the line alone.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 40, 'Ekene-6''s primary window spans 821 days, over which the rate falls from 90 stb/d to 43.7344375460616 stb/d. Why does the workflow record that span?', '["Because the ratio of window length to forecast length sets the confidence interval on the booked EUR", "Because a window shorter than 900 days cannot support a hyperbolic fit under the tier''s conventions", "Because forecast length must be a multiple of it", "Because it is the evidence supporting the fitted b"]'::jsonb, 3, 'b needs observed change in the decline to be identified, so how much decline the window actually contains is the argument for the exponent you booked. A reviewer cannot judge the b without it.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 41, 'You enter 461475.535264973 for a capstone field graded at 461709.132532792 stb with a tolerance of 2000 stb. What happens, and what should you still do?', '["It fails, because the graded value is the closed-form total and the roll-up total is a different quantity", "It passes, and you record which of the two totals you entered", "It passes, and the distinction does not matter", "It fails, because a difference of 233.597267818404 stb exceeds the precision the grader accepts on a field total"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two totals differ by 233.597267818404 stb, comfortably inside the tolerance, so either passes. Passing is not the same as knowing what you reported, and the report has to name the convention.'),
  ('dca', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 42, 'Why does the Expert tier reproduce worked examples printed in the standard references?', '["Because published examples carry noise", "Because reserves auditors require every booking to cite a published analogue from the literature", "Because a tool has to be shown to compute what it claims, against a citable check", "Because the b values used in the literature are the only ones a reserves committee will accept without argument"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Real work has no answer key, so published examples with printed inputs and printed answers stand in for one. Citing the reference and the page is what turns a private check into evidence someone else can repeat.');
